description:
calendar.rs:117-124, event.rs:258-265, participant_identity.rs:100-107 — all three /set create paths use the same workaround for the fact that the typed Calendar / CalendarEvent / ParticipantIdentity structs require an 'id' field even though the spec forbids the client from supplying one. The handler force-inserts a placeholder:

  let obj_with_id = match obj_val {
      Value::Object(mut m) => {
          m.entry('id')
              .or_insert_with(|| Value::String('placeholder'.to_owned()));
          Value::Object(m)
      }
      other => other,
  };
  let cal: Calendar = serde_json::from_value(obj_with_id)?;

After deserialize, the placeholder 'id' is meaningless — the backend's create_object assigns the real id (memory.rs:672-681). The placeholder exists only to satisfy serde's required-field check.

Problems:

1. Three independent copies. Adding a new createable type means a fourth.
2. The handler types Calendar/CalendarEvent/ParticipantIdentity have required 'id' which is the WRONG shape for a create wire payload. The type's contract should express 'this object always has an id' (because that is true post-create), but creating one from-scratch needs an 'id-less' variant.
3. Compare the canonical jmap-mail-server (mail-server email.rs constants like PLACEHOLDER_BLOB_ID, commit bae10ae) which made this pattern explicit by extracting a constant — calendars-server has not adopted that pattern, so the literal 'placeholder' appears three times.
4. If a future field validator on Calendar/CalendarEvent rejects 'placeholder' as an invalid id format, every /set create breaks.

Two cleaner shapes, pick one:

(a) Extract PLACEHOLDER_ID = 'placeholder' as a pub(crate) const in helpers.rs and use it everywhere. Mirrors mail-server's PLACEHOLDER_BLOB_ID pattern.
(b) Define a serde-side CreatedCalendar / CreatedCalendarEvent struct in jmap-calendars-types with id: Option<Id>, deserialize the wire payload into that, then translate to the canonical Calendar with a sentinel id. Heavier lift, larger blast radius.

The (a) fix is what the canonical sibling adopted (bd:JMAP-mail-server bae10ae). Propagation here is the cookie-cutter consistency story.

P3 because nothing actively breaks today; this is teach-by-example cleanup that also gets you free if you do the cookie-cutter sweep.
